  "question": {
    "text": "Consider the vectors\n$\\vec{x}=\\hat{i}+2 \\hat{j}+3 \\hat{k}, \\quad \\vec{y}=2 \\hat{i}+3 \\hat{j}+\\hat{k}, \\quad \\text { and } \\quad \\vec{z}=3 \\hat{i}+\\hat{j}+2 \\hat{k}$\nFor two distinct positive real numbers $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, define\n$\\vec{X}=\\alpha \\vec{x}+\\beta \\vec{y}-\\vec{z}, \\quad \\vec{Y}=\\alpha \\vec{y}+\\beta \\vec{z}-\\vec{x}, \\quad \\text { and } \\quad \\vec{Z}=\\alpha \\vec{z}+\\beta \\vec{x}-\\vec{y}$\nIf the vectors $\\vec{X}, \\vec{Y}$, and $\\vec{Z}$ lie in a plane, the value of $\\alpha+\\beta-3$ is ______ .",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "-2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\Rightarrow\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\\alpha & \\beta & -1 \\\\ -1 & \\alpha & \\beta \\\\ \\beta & -1 & \\alpha\\end{array}\\right| \\underbrace{\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right|}_{-0}=0$\n$\\begin{aligned} & \\Rightarrow\\left(\\alpha^3+\\beta^3-1\\right)-(-\\alpha \\beta-\\alpha \\beta-\\alpha \\beta)=0 \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\alpha^3+\\beta^3+3 \\alpha \\beta=1 \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\alpha^3+\\beta^3+(-1)^3=3(\\alpha)(\\beta)(-1) \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\alpha+\\beta-1=0\\end{aligned}$\nSo, $\\alpha+\\beta-3=-2$",
    "image": null
  },
